1. The code
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

All R sources in this package are original work, licensed under the Apache
License, Version 2.0 (see LICENSE).

No source is copied from 'energyRt'. The package uses 'energyRt' only through
its exported API -- the newTechnology(), newTrade(), newACLine(),
lossTranches(), ... constructors, plus getData() and load_scenario(). No S4
class is defined or redefined here.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. IMPORTANT: reneuro does not run without an AGPL-3 dependency
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

'energyRt' is licensed under AGPL-3, and reneuro lists it in Imports. R links
imported namespaces into a single process, so a distribution of reneuro
TOGETHER WITH 'energyRt' -- which is the only form in which reneuro is usable --
is a combined work that must be conveyed under AGPL-3.

Apache-2.0 is one-way compatible with AGPL-3, so that combination is lawful.
But do not read "Apache-2.0" as "permissive in practice": it is not, for anyone
shipping the working package.

What the Apache-2.0 licence does buy is that the conversion code itself -- the
PyPSA netCDF reader, the reimplementation of PyPSA's apply_line_types(), the
Kirchhoff cycle-basis construction, and the piecewise-linear loss tranches --
may be reused WITHOUT 'energyRt' under permissive terms.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. The data
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The converted models shipped in data/ derive from PyPSA-Eur
(https://github.com/PyPSA/pypsa-eur) and are licensed under the Open Database
License v1.0 (ODbL-1.0), required by the share-alike terms of the
OpenStreetMap-derived inputs. This is independent of the code licence above.

See inst/LICENSE.note for per-input provenance, including which inputs were
excluded from the rebuild and which carry unclear upstream terms.
